time, and summary of the received data.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>authenticated api request</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>f3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</b:SourceGuid><b:State>AutoGenerated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>9fb9158b-4329-4917-b6c5-2c961596a2a4</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>R6</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>S2dac612cd-2bb2-44c7-9b13-1bd23b9cdfdf5317d084-e63b-4b34-b8e5-72a7e11cb6ddf3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>b8cca8a7-a71e-4495-8b8b-37910620c923</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>5317d084-e63b-4b34-b8e5-72a7e11cb6dd</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>139</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>dac612cd-2bb2-44c7-9b13-1bd23b9cdfdf:5317d084-e63b-4b34-b8e5-72a7e11cb6dd:f3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing the EE1 Registered User (Browser) Process</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Spoofing is when a process or entity is something other than its claimed identity. Examples include substituting a process, a file, website or a network address.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>EE1 Registered User (Browser) may be spoofed by an attacker and this may lead to information disclosure by External communication channel. Consider using a standard authentication mechanism to identify the destination process.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>password reset token</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>dac612cd-2bb2-44c7-9b13-1bd23b9cdfdf</b:SourceGuid><b:State>AutoGenerated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>f3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>S2</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>T1dac612cd-2bb2-44c7-9b13-1bd23b9cdfdf5317d084-e63b-4b34-b8e5-72a7e11cb6ddf3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>b8cca8a7-a71e-4495-8b8b-37910620c923</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>5317d084-e63b-4b34-b8e5-72a7e11cb6dd</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>140</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>dac612cd-2bb2-44c7-9b13-1bd23b9cdfdf:5317d084-e63b-4b34-b8e5-72a7e11cb6dd:f3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Potential Lack of Input Validation for EE1 Registered User (Browser)</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Tampering</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Tampering is the act of altering the bits. Tampering with a process involves changing bits in the running process. Similarly, Tampering with a data flow involves changing bits on the wire or between two running processes.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Data flowing across password reset token may be tampered with by an attacker. This may lead to a denial of service attack against EE1 Registered User (Browser) or an elevation of privilege attack against EE1 Registered User (Browser) or an information disclosure by EE1 Registered User (Browser). Failure to verify that input is as expected is a root cause of a very large number of exploitable issues. Consider all paths and the way they handle data. Verify that all input is verified for correctness using an approved list input validation approach.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>password reset token</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>dac612cd-2bb2-44c7-9b13-1bd23b9cdfdf</b:SourceGuid><b:State>AutoGenerated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>f3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>T1</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>R6dac612cd-2bb2-44c7-9b13-1bd23b9cdfdf5317d084-e63b-4b34-b8e5-72a7e11cb6ddf3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>b8cca8a7-a71e-4495-8b8b-37910620c923</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>5317d084-e63b-4b34-b8e5-72a7e11cb6dd</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>141</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>dac612cd-2bb2-44c7-9b13-1bd23b9cdfdf:5317d084-e63b-4b34-b8e5-72a7e11cb6dd:f3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Potential Data Repudiation by EE1 Registered User (Browser)</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Repudiation</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Repudiation threats involve an adversary denying that something happened.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>EE1 Registered User (Browser) claims that it did not receive data from a source outside the trust boundary. Consider using logging or auditing to record the source, time, and summary of the received data.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>password reset token</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>dac612cd-2bb2-44c7-9b13-1bd23b9cdfdf</b:SourceGuid><b:State>AutoGenerated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>f3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>R6</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatShortDescription i:nil="true"/><b:Wide>false</b:Wide></a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:KeyValueOfstringThreatpc_P0_PhOB><a:Key>I6dac612cd-2bb2-44c7-9b13-1bd23b9cdfdf5317d084-e63b-4b34-b8e5-72a7e11cb6ddf3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</a:Key><a:Value xmlns:b="http://schemas.datacontract.org/2004/07/ThreatModeling.KnowledgeBase"><b:ChangedBy i:nil="true"/><b:DrawingSurfaceGuid>b8cca8a7-a71e-4495-8b8b-37910620c923</b:DrawingSurfaceGuid><b:FlowGuid>5317d084-e63b-4b34-b8e5-72a7e11cb6dd</b:FlowGuid><b:Id>142</b:Id><b:InteractionKey>dac612cd-2bb2-44c7-9b13-1bd23b9cdfdf:5317d084-e63b-4b34-b8e5-72a7e11cb6dd:f3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</b:InteractionKey><b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/><b:ModifiedAt>0001-01-01T00:00:00</b:ModifiedAt><b:Priority>High</b:Priority><b:Properties><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Title</a:Key><a:Value>Data Flow Sniffing</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key><a:Value>Information Disclosure</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Information disclosure happens when the information can be read by an unauthorized party.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key><a:Value>Data flowing across password reset token may be sniffed by an attacker. Depending on what type of data an attacker can read, it may be used to attack other parts of the system or simply be a disclosure of information leading to compliance violations. Consider encrypting the data flow.</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key><a:Value>password reset token</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:KeyValueOfstringstring><a:Key>Priority</a:Key><a:Value>High</a:Value></a:KeyValueOfstringstring></b:Properties><b:SourceGuid>dac612cd-2bb2-44c7-9b13-1bd23b9cdfdf</b:SourceGuid><b:State>AutoGenerated</b:State><b:StateInformation i:nil="true"/><b:TargetGuid>f3765703-9d86-4cd4-8e1f-653327b0acef</b:TargetGuid><b:Title i:nil="true"/><b:TypeId>I6</b:TypeId><b:Upgraded>false</b:Upgraded><b:UserThreatCategory i:nil="true"/><b:UserThreatDescription i